PREFACE

  Directive 12 of 2004

  Directive on the protection of natural persons with regard to the processing of personal data

  Directive of the Assembly of the Hesperian Union and of the Hesperian Council on the protection of natural persons with regard to the processing of personal data and on the free movement of such data within the Union, laying down the rights of data subjects, the obligations of controllers and processors, and the arrangements for supervision by independent authorities.

PREAMBLE

  Having regard to the Treaty establishing the Hesperian Union, and in particular Article 41 thereof,

  Having regard to the proposal from the Hesperian Commission,

  Having regard to the opinion of the Union Economic and Social Committee,

  Whereas:

  (1) The processing of personal data should be designed to serve natural persons; the principles and rules on the protection of such persons with regard to the processing of their data should respect their fundamental rights and freedoms, whatever their nationality or residence.

  (2) The internal market of the Union comprises an area without internal frontiers in which the free movement of data is ensured; divergent levels of protection between member states impede the flow of personal data across the Union and distort competition.

  (3) In order to remove those obstacles, the level of protection of the rights and freedoms of natural persons with regard to the processing of personal data should be equivalent in all member states, and this Directive seeks that equivalence while allowing the free movement of such data.

  (4) A controller or processor established in more than one member state should remain subject to a coherent set of obligations, and supervisory authorities should cooperate to secure the consistent application of this Directive.

  (5) The processing of special categories of data, revealing matters of a particularly sensitive nature in relation to fundamental rights, merits specific protection, and such processing should be permitted only in the circumstances expressly provided for in this Directive.

  (6) Each member state should provide for one or more independent public authorities charged with monitoring the application of this Directive, in order to protect the fundamental rights of natural persons and to facilitate the free flow of personal data within the Union.

  (7) Since the objectives of this Directive cannot be sufficiently achieved by the member states acting alone and can be better achieved at the level of the Union, the Union may adopt measures in accordance with the principle of subsidiarity; this Directive does not go beyond what is necessary to achieve those objectives.

  THE ASSEMBLY OF THE HESPERIAN UNION AND THE HESPERIAN COUNCIL, Having regard to the Treaty establishing the Hesperian Union, HAS ADOPTED THIS DIRECTIVE:

BODY

CHAPTER 1 - GENERAL PROVISIONS

  ARTICLE 1 - Subject matter and objectives

    PARAGRAPH (1)

      This Directive lays down rules relating to the protection of natural persons with regard to the processing of personal data and rules relating to the free movement of personal data within the Union.

    PARAGRAPH (2)

      This Directive protects the fundamental rights and freedoms of natural persons and in particular their right to the protection of personal data.

    PARAGRAPH (3)

      The free movement of personal data within the Union shall be neither restricted nor prohibited for reasons connected with the protection of natural persons with regard to the processing of personal data.

  ARTICLE 2 - Material scope

    PARAGRAPH (1)

      This Directive applies to the processing of personal data wholly or partly by automated means, and to the processing otherwise than by automated means of personal data which form part of a filing system or are intended to form part of a filing system.

    PARAGRAPH (2)

      This Directive does not apply to the processing of personal data—

      POINT (a)

        in the course of an activity which falls outside the competence of the Union;

      POINT (b)

        by a natural person in the course of a purely personal or household activity; or

      POINT (c)

        by competent authorities for the purposes of the prevention, investigation or prosecution of criminal offences, which shall be governed by a separate instrument of the Union.

  ARTICLE 3 - Definitions

    For the purposes of this Directive, the following definitions apply:

    POINT (a)

      "personal data" means any information relating to an identified or identifiable natural person, and an identifiable natural person is one who can be identified, directly or indirectly, in particular by reference to an identifier such as a name, an identification number or one or more factors specific to that person;

    POINT (b)

      "processing" means any operation performed on personal data, whether or not by automated means, such as collection, recording, storage, alteration, retrieval, disclosure, erasure or destruction;

    POINT (c)

      "controller" means the natural or legal person, public authority or other body which, alone or jointly with others, determines the purposes and means of the processing of personal data;

    POINT (d)

      "processor" means a natural or legal person, public authority or other body which processes personal data on behalf of the controller;

    POINT (e)

      "data subject" means the identified or identifiable natural person to whom the personal data relate;

    POINT (f)

      "consent" means any freely given, specific, informed and unambiguous indication of the data subject's wishes by which the data subject signifies agreement to the processing of personal data relating to that person;

    POINT (g)

      "supervisory authority" means an independent public authority established by a member state under Chapter 3;

    POINT (h)

      "filing system" means any structured set of personal data which is accessible according to specified criteria, whether centralised, decentralised or dispersed on a functional or geographical basis.

  ARTICLE 4 - Applicable law

    PARAGRAPH (1)

      This Directive applies to the processing of personal data in the context of the activities of an establishment of a controller or processor in the Union, regardless of whether the processing takes place within the Union.

    PARAGRAPH (2)

      Where the law of a member state applies by virtue of public international law, this Directive applies to a controller not established in the Union but in a place where the national law of that member state applies.

CHAPTER 2 - PRINCIPLES AND OBLIGATIONS

  ARTICLE 5 - Principles relating to processing

    PARAGRAPH (1)

      Personal data shall be—

      POINT (a)

        processed lawfully, fairly and in a transparent manner in relation to the data subject;

      POINT (b)

        collected for specified, explicit and legitimate purposes and not further processed in a manner incompatible with those purposes;

      POINT (c)

        adequate, relevant and limited to what is necessary in relation to the purposes for which they are processed;

      POINT (d)

        accurate and, where necessary, kept up to date;

      POINT (e)

        kept in a form which permits identification of data subjects for no longer than is necessary for the purposes for which the data are processed; and

      POINT (f)

        processed in a manner that ensures appropriate security of the personal data.

    PARAGRAPH (2)

      The controller is responsible for, and shall be able to demonstrate compliance with, paragraph (1).

  ARTICLE 6 - Lawfulness of processing

    PARAGRAPH (1)

      Processing is lawful only if and to the extent that at least one of the following applies—

      POINT (a)

        the data subject has given consent to the processing for one or more specific purposes;

      POINT (b)

        processing is necessary for the performance of a contract to which the data subject is party;

      POINT (c)

        processing is necessary for compliance with a legal obligation to which the controller is subject;

      POINT (d)

        processing is necessary in order to protect the vital interests of the data subject or of another natural person;

      POINT (e)

        processing is necessary for the performance of a task carried out in the public interest; or

      POINT (f)

        processing is necessary for the purposes of the legitimate interests pursued by the controller, except where such interests are overridden by the interests or fundamental rights of the data subject.

    PARAGRAPH (2)

      Where processing for a purpose other than that for which the data were collected is not based on the data subject's consent, the controller shall ascertain whether the further processing is compatible with the original purpose.

  ARTICLE 7 - Special categories of personal data

    PARAGRAPH (1)

      The processing of personal data revealing racial or ethnic origin, political opinions, religious or philosophical beliefs, or trade union membership, and the processing of data concerning health or a natural person's sexual life, shall be prohibited.

    PARAGRAPH (2)

      Paragraph (1) does not apply where the data subject has given explicit consent to the processing, or where processing is necessary for the purposes of carrying out obligations in the field of employment or social protection law, or for reasons of substantial public interest provided for by the law of the Union or of a member state.

  ARTICLE 8 - Information to be provided to the data subject

    PARAGRAPH (1)

      Where personal data are collected from the data subject, the controller shall, at the time the data are obtained, provide the data subject with the identity of the controller, the purposes of the processing, and the legal basis for the processing.

    PARAGRAPH (2)

      The controller shall further provide any information necessary to ensure fair and transparent processing, having regard to the specific circumstances in which the data are processed.

  ARTICLE 9 - Right of access

    The data subject shall have the right to obtain from the controller confirmation as to whether personal data concerning that person are being processed and, where that is the case, access to the data and information as to the purposes of the processing, the categories of data concerned and the recipients to whom the data have been disclosed.

  ARTICLE 10 - Right to rectification and erasure

    PARAGRAPH (1)

      The data subject shall have the right to obtain from the controller, without undue delay, the rectification of inaccurate personal data concerning that person.

    PARAGRAPH (2)

      The data subject shall have the right to obtain the erasure of personal data concerning that person where the data are no longer necessary for the purposes for which they were collected, where consent is withdrawn, or where the data have been unlawfully processed.

  ARTICLE 11 - Right to object

    The data subject shall have the right to object, on grounds relating to that person's particular situation, to the processing of personal data which is based on the performance of a task carried out in the public interest or on the legitimate interests of the controller, and the controller shall no longer process the data unless it demonstrates compelling legitimate grounds which override the interests and rights of the data subject.

  ARTICLE 12 - Automated individual decision-making

    The data subject shall have the right not to be subject to a decision based solely on automated processing which produces legal effects concerning that person or similarly significantly affects that person, save where the decision is authorised by the law of the Union or of a member state which lays down suitable safeguards.

  ARTICLE 13 - Security of processing

    PARAGRAPH (1)

      The controller and the processor shall implement appropriate technical and organisational measures to ensure a level of security appropriate to the risk, having regard to the state of the art and the nature of the processing.

    PARAGRAPH (2)

      In assessing the appropriate level of security, account shall be taken of the risks presented by processing, in particular the accidental or unlawful destruction, loss, alteration or unauthorised disclosure of personal data.

  ARTICLE 14 - Processing under the authority of the controller

    The processor and any person acting under the authority of the controller or the processor who has access to personal data shall not process those data except on instructions from the controller, unless required to do so by the law of the Union or of a member state.

  ARTICLE 15 - Notification of a personal data breach

    PARAGRAPH (1)

      In the case of a personal data breach, the controller shall without undue delay, and where feasible not later than seventy-two hours after having become aware of it, notify the breach to the competent supervisory authority.

    PARAGRAPH (2)

      Where the breach is likely to result in a high risk to the rights and freedoms of natural persons, the controller shall communicate the breach to the data subject without undue delay.

  ARTICLE 16 - Data protection by design

    The controller shall, both at the time of the determination of the means of processing and at the time of the processing itself, implement appropriate measures designed to give effect to the principles of this Directive and to integrate the necessary safeguards into the processing.

CHAPTER 3 - SUPERVISION, REMEDIES AND FINAL PROVISIONS

  ARTICLE 17 - Supervisory authority

    PARAGRAPH (1)

      Each member state shall provide for one or more independent public authorities to be responsible for monitoring the application of this Directive.

    PARAGRAPH (2)

      Each supervisory authority shall act with complete independence in performing its tasks and exercising its powers, and shall remain free from external influence, whether direct or indirect.

  ARTICLE 18 - Tasks and powers of the supervisory authority

    PARAGRAPH (1)

      Each supervisory authority shall monitor and enforce the application of this Directive, promote public awareness of the risks and rights relating to processing, and handle complaints lodged by data subjects.

    PARAGRAPH (2)

      Each supervisory authority shall have the power to obtain access to all personal data and to all information necessary for the performance of its tasks, to order the controller to bring processing into compliance, and to impose a temporary or definitive limitation, including a ban, on processing.

  ARTICLE 19 - Cooperation between supervisory authorities

    Supervisory authorities shall provide each other with relevant information and mutual assistance in order to implement and apply this Directive in a consistent manner, and shall put in place measures for effective cooperation, including joint operations where processing affects data subjects in more than one member state.

  ARTICLE 20 - Right to an effective remedy

    Without prejudice to any available administrative remedy, each data subject shall have the right to an effective judicial remedy where that person considers that rights under this Directive have been infringed as a result of processing which does not comply with this Directive.

  ARTICLE 21 - Liability and compensation

    Any person who has suffered material or non-material damage as a result of an infringement of this Directive shall have the right to receive compensation from the controller or processor for the damage suffered, and a controller or processor shall be exempt from liability only if it proves that it is not in any way responsible for the event giving rise to the damage.

  ARTICLE 22 - Penalties

    Member states shall lay down the rules on penalties applicable to infringements of the national provisions adopted pursuant to this Directive, and shall take all measures necessary to ensure that they are implemented; the penalties provided for shall be effective, proportionate and dissuasive.

  ARTICLE 23 - Transfers to third countries

    PARAGRAPH (1)

      A transfer of personal data to a country outside the Union may take place only where the Commission has decided that the country ensures an adequate level of protection, or where the controller has provided appropriate safeguards.

    PARAGRAPH (2)

      In the absence of an adequacy decision or appropriate safeguards, a transfer may take place only on one of the specific grounds provided for by the law of the Union, including the explicit consent of the data subject.

  ARTICLE 24 - Transposition

    PARAGRAPH (1)

      Member states shall bring into force the laws, regulations and administrative provisions necessary to comply with this Directive by 31 December 2006, and shall immediately inform the Commission thereof.

    PARAGRAPH (2)

      When member states adopt those measures, they shall contain a reference to this Directive or be accompanied by such a reference on the occasion of their official publication, and the methods of making such a reference shall be laid down by the member states.

    PARAGRAPH (3)

      Member states shall communicate to the Commission the text of the main provisions of national law which they adopt in the field governed by this Directive.

  ARTICLE 25 - Entry into force

    This Directive shall enter into force on the twentieth day following that of its publication in the Official Journal of the Hesperian Union, and is addressed to the member states.
